title: Qubole Cross-Account Trust Abuse
page: breaches/qubole-cross-account-trust.html
as-of: 2026-11-23
promise: Customer AWS roles that still trusted an account associated with Qubole let an actor read secrets and download S3 data; how it got the vendor's credentials has not been disclosed.

== At a glance
layout: stats
stat: 14 to 26 | October: the window of role assumptions Wiz observed | Wiz and Sentra published on November 9, 2026
stat: Dozen+ | organizations hit, according to Sentra | Wiz says multiple and gave no count
Every request Wiz attributed carried an External ID and succeeded. Wiz and Sentra name no actor. Disclosure: the page draws mainly on Wiz; Google acquired Wiz in March 2026, and the site's author works at Wiz.
notes:
Start with the page's headline facts, and with who stands behind each one.

Between October 14 and October 26, Wiz observed a threat actor assuming roles in customer AWS environments with credentials from an account it describes as associated with Qubole. Wiz and Sentra published their accounts on November 9, 2026.

More than a dozen organizations were hit, according to Sentra; Wiz says multiple and declined to give a number. The page's text names no victim organization, and this deck names none.

Every AssumeRole request Wiz attributed to the actor carried an External ID, and every one succeeded. Stage 03 explains why that did not stop it.

Neither Wiz nor Sentra names the threat actor. Wiz says the targeting appears opportunistic and does not match typical state-backed priorities, while noting it has no direct insight into motive. An extortion group's later claim is unverified.

Say the disclosure aloud as the page gives it. The page draws mainly on research published by Wiz, whose incident response team investigated with Sentra and with Mandiant, which is part of Google. Google acquired Wiz in March 2026, and the site's author works at Wiz. Sentra, the page's second main source, is a cloud data security company. Every source is linked so you can read it directly.

== What happened, and when
layout: timeline
event: March 2025 | Idera, which acquired Qubole in 2020, says it sold Qubole to Gsudoers
event: October 14 | Wiz's window opens: role assumptions with the first of two IAM users
event: October 18 | A second IAM user, which also carries Qubole's own traffic
event: October 26 | The end of the window Wiz observed
event: November 9 | Wiz and Sentra publish their accounts
event: November 11 | n0n claims the intrusion; its claims are unverified
Dates are 2026 unless shown. The researchers have not said whether the activity has stopped. A separate October 8 to 11 cluster, not linked by Wiz, has its own slide. Spacing is not to scale.
notes:
Every date here is one the page gives.

Qubole is a data lake platform. Idera acquired it in 2020 and, by Idera's account, sold it to Gsudoers in March 2025.

From October 14 the actor used the first of two IAM users in the account Wiz describes as associated with Qubole. From October 18 it also used a second IAM user in the same account, one that also carries legitimate Qubole platform traffic. Wiz's window of role assumptions runs to October 26.

Wiz and Sentra published on November 9. On November 11 an extortion group calling itself n0n claimed the Qubole intrusion on its leak site. Its claims are unverified.

The researchers did not say whether the credentials have since been disabled or whether the activity has stopped. Qubole had made no public statement as of the sources' dates.

Wiz also reports a separate cluster from October 8 to October 11 that it has not been able to link to the role assumptions. It is not on this line, and it is not a stage of the chain. It has its own slide.

The markers are evenly spaced, so do not read the gaps between them as durations.

== Who reported what
layout: table
row: Source | What it reports, per the page
row: Wiz | The role assumptions and their indicators; multiple victims, no count
row: Sentra | Detected the activity and told Wiz; more than a dozen organizations hit
row: Mandiant | Investigated jointly with Wiz and Sentra
row: AWS | Its services "were not compromised by this issue and operated as designed"
row: Qubole | No public statement as of the sources' dates
Disclosure: Mandiant is part of Google, which acquired Wiz in March 2026, and the site's author works at Wiz.
notes:
Each part of this story belongs to a source, and the page keeps them apart.

Wiz describes the role assumptions, the account and IAM users involved, and the indicators. It says multiple organizations were affected and declined to give a number.

Sentra detected the activity and reported it to Wiz, which led to a joint investigation with Wiz and Mandiant. Sentra counts more than a dozen organizations hit, and reports details Wiz does not. Those details stay labelled as Sentra's on later slides.

AWS told The Register and BleepingComputer that its services "were not compromised by this issue and operated as designed." That statement is AWS's, not Wiz's. Keep it attributed when you repeat it.

Qubole had made no public statement as of the sources' dates.

Say the disclosure as the page gives it. Mandiant is part of Google. Google acquired Wiz in March 2026, and the site's author works at Wiz. Sentra is a cloud data security company.

The page describes the source account as one Wiz says is associated with Qubole. It does not say that Qubole itself was breached, and nobody has disclosed how the actor got the account's credentials.

== Two reports the page keeps off the chain
layout: compare
col: October 8 to 11, not linked by Wiz | Customer keys Wiz believes were created for Qubole integrations | All tested within one hour on October 8 | A small number of victim environments compromised | Where the keys came from: not said
col: n0n's claim, unverified | Claimed the intrusion on its leak site, November 11 | Its claims about how much data it holds are unverified | Press, Cyberint: Qubole may be a source of other breaches; no link confirmed
Cyberint found no Qubole-specific markers in the files released.
notes:
The page reports two things beside the chain without making them part of it. Keep both apart when you present.

First, Wiz reports a separate cluster of activity that it has not been able to link to the role assumptions. From October 8 to October 11, an actor used AWS access keys owned by customers, which Wiz believes were created for Qubole integrations. On October 8 it tested all of those keys within a single hour, and in a small number of cases it went on to compromise the victim's cloud environment. Wiz does not say where the keys came from. The Hacker News notes that Qubole also supports onboarding with access keys rather than a role.

Second, on November 11 an extortion group calling itself n0n claimed the Qubole intrusion on its leak site. Wiz added a note to its post reporting the claim, without saying whether the claim is genuine. n0n's claims about how much data it holds are unverified.

Press reports and Check Point's Cyberint team have since raised Qubole as a possible source of breaches that other companies disclosed at third-party providers. Cyberint found no Qubole-specific markers in the files released, and neither the researchers nor those companies have confirmed a link. The page's text does not name those companies, and neither does this deck.

== The kill chain on one slide
layout: flow
step: 01 | Resource Development | Server and VPN | T1583.003
step: 02 | Initial Access | Vendor identity | T1078.004
step: 03 | Initial Access | Dormant roles | T1199
step: 04 | Discovery | Enumeration | T1580
step: 05 | Credential Access | Secrets read | T1555.006
step: 06 | Initial Access | Secrets tested | T1078.004
step: 07 | Collection | S3 data taken | T1530
Arrows show the page's stage order. The credentials tested at stage 06 were the victims', not the vendor's.
notes:
Here is the whole chain on one slide: the page's seven stages, each with its phase and its first-listed ATT&CK technique.

Stage 01 is the actor's infrastructure: an OVH server, Mullvad VPN addresses and the TruffleHog secret scanner. Stage 02 is the vendor's identity: working credentials for two IAM users in the account Qubole's platform uses to reach customers. Stage 03 is the role assumption itself, into integration roles that customers had never removed. Stage 04 is discovery inside each victim account. Stage 05 is credential access: secrets read from Lambda, Secrets Manager and Parameter Store. Stage 06 is TruffleHog testing those secrets against AWS, Entra ID and Okta. Stage 07 is collection: S3 objects downloaded, sometimes whole buckets.

The page files stage 06 under Initial Access and maps it to the same technique as stage 02, Valid Accounts: Cloud Accounts. The difference is whose credentials they were. At stage 02 they were the vendor's; at stage 06 they were the victims' own.

The separate October 8 to 11 activity is not a stage, and it is not on this slide.

== A server, VPN exits and a secret scanner
layout: stage
stage: 01
phase: Resource Development
technique: T1583.003 | Acquire Infrastructure: Virtual Private Server
technique: T1588.002 | Obtain Capabilities: Tool
fact: Primary host | an OVH VPS, 57.129.54[.]120, October 15 to 26
fact: VPN | three Mullvad addresses, which are shared exits
fact: Tool | TruffleHog, which legitimate security teams also run
fact: Staged | sso_agent.iso, a bootable Linux image; Wiz saw no deployment
Wiz believes the image is meant to be booted remotely on a victim machine, and cannot confirm if or how it has been used.
notes:
Wiz's indicator tables lay out the infrastructure. An OVH virtual private server at 57.129.54[.]120 was the actor's primary address for role assumption, enumeration and credential harvesting, seen from October 15 to October 26. An OVH IPv6 address, 2001:41d0:70d[:]7800::, carried credential enumeration and verification from October 16 to October 24.

Wiz ties three more addresses to Mullvad VPN: 45.83.220[.]211 on October 14 and 15, 185.209.199[.]73 from October 18 to 21, and 89.37.63[.]175 on October 25 only. Wiz notes these are shared VPN exits, so activity from one of them alone is not conclusive.

To check the credentials it harvested, the actor used TruffleHog, an open-source secret scanner that, as Wiz points out, legitimate security teams also run.

Wiz also found sso_agent.iso hosted on the OVH server. Once started it begins a 90-minute clock, gathers system information and uploads its network interfaces and its disk and partition lists to 57.129.54[.]120. The page lists its polling port as TCP 23458. Wiz has not identified any deployment of the image.

The page maps this stage to T1583.003, with T1588.002 and T1608.001, Stage Capabilities: Upload Malware, as tags. The slide shows the first two.

== Working credentials for two IAM users in the vendor's account
layout: stage
stage: 02
phase: Initial Access
technique: T1078.004 | Valid Accounts: Cloud Accounts
fact: Source account | 805246085872, which Wiz describes as associated with Qubole
fact: First IAM user | from October 14
fact: Second IAM user | from October 18; also carries legitimate Qubole traffic
fact: Not disclosed | how the actor got them, or whether they were since disabled
To AWS, every role assumption came from the vendor's trusted account.
notes:
Wiz lists AWS account 805246085872 as the source of every role assumption it observed. It describes the account as associated with Qubole, and says it observes Qubole's platform using it to assume roles in customer environments.

From October 14 the actor used the IAM user AIDA3W7DIFLYOLTK63SFS. From October 18 it also used a second IAM user in the same account, AIDAJYRRH5Y3A7AAJM2HW, which also carries legitimate Qubole platform traffic from AWS infrastructure. The hunting slide later in the deck lists both IDs.

How the actor obtained those credentials has not been disclosed, and the researchers did not say whether the credentials have since been disabled. Sentra writes that the attacker went quiet without ever losing access.

The page maps this stage to T1078.004, Valid Accounts: Cloud Accounts. ATT&CK lists that technique under Stealth as well as Initial Access, and the page says both fit here. No source reports the actor tampering with logs or security tools, so the page maps nothing to Defense Impairment.

== What is said, and not said, about the vendor's credentials
layout: compare
col: How each source puts it | Sentra's headline: a stolen vendor key | BleepingComputer and SecurityWeek: compromised credentials | Wiz: describes the IAM users neither way
col: Not disclosed by Wiz, Sentra, AWS or Qubole | How the actor got the credentials | How it held a valid External ID for each role | Whether the credentials have since been disabled
Wiz advises SaaS providers to use short-lived credentials, not long-lived IAM user keys. Wiz does not say long-lived keys are how this happened.
notes:
Be precise here, because the sources use different words.

Sentra's headline calls it a stolen vendor key. BleepingComputer and SecurityWeek describe the credentials as compromised. Wiz does not describe the IAM users either way; it says the account is associated with Qubole and that Qubole's platform uses it.

Three things remain undisclosed. Wiz, Sentra, AWS and Qubole have not said how the actor got the vendor's credentials, how it held a valid External ID for each role, or whether the credentials have since been disabled.

Wiz's advice to SaaS providers is to assume customer roles from workload roles with short-lived credentials rather than long-lived IAM user keys. That describes the safer design. Wiz does not say that long-lived keys are how this happened, so do not present it as the cause.

Sentra says the actor read the vendor's own tokens and ExternalIds out of victims' Secrets Manager. That happened after the actor was inside, so the page says it does not explain the first entry.

The next slide explains, in general terms, how a role lets a third party into your account.

== How a third party reaches your account through a role
layout: background
source: AWS IAM User Guide, access to AWS accounts owned by third parties | https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/id_roles_common-scenarios_third-party.html
credit: Also from the AWS STS API Reference, AssumeRole (docs.aws.amazon.com)
A role has a trust policy, saying who can assume it, and a permission policy, saying what it allows. For a third party, you name its AWS account as the principal. Its identities call AssumeRole and receive temporary credentials.
notes:
This is a background slide. It explains a mechanism in general, from AWS's documentation, and says nothing about the incident.

AWS describes delegating access to a third party with a role you create in your own account. Like any IAM role, it has two policies. The trust policy specifies who can assume the role; for a third party, it names that party's AWS account as the principal, which allows identities from that account to assume the role. The permission policy specifies what the role allows someone to do and what resources they can access. AWS warns that third parties can access any resource you specify in that policy.

A member of the third party's account then calls the AWS Security Token Service AssumeRole API and receives temporary security credentials for your account. AWS advises against giving a third party an IAM user and its long-term credentials in your account, and recommends a role and its temporary credentials instead.

The STS API Reference adds that a cross-account role is usually set up to trust everyone in an account, and that the role session name is visible to, and can be logged by, the account that owns the role.

Also from the AWS STS API Reference, AssumeRole: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/STS/latest/APIReference/API_AssumeRole.html

== AssumeRole into roles nobody had removed, and every request succeeded
layout: stage
stage: 03
phase: Initial Access
technique: T1199 | Trusted Relationship
fact: AssumeRole | with an External ID; every attributed request succeeded (Wiz)
fact: Session names | quiam-enum, quiam-scope-full
fact: Why it worked | trust policies left on dormant roles, with no network condition
fact: Detect | AssumeRole from 805246085872 from a non-AWS address
Wiz: "The trust policies were still in place, so the requests succeeded."
notes:
To connect Qubole to an AWS account, a customer creates an IAM role whose trust policy lets the Qubole account assume it, on condition that the caller presents an External ID. Every AssumeRole request Wiz attributed to the actor included one, and every one succeeded. The page maps this to T1199, Trusted Relationship.

Wiz's explanation: "The trust policies were still in place, so the requests succeeded." Many organizations had left those policies active after they stopped using the platform, Wiz told The Register. Most of the roles were otherwise unused, and in a majority of the environments Wiz saw, the actor's requests were the only AssumeRole activity from the vendor account in October. Sentra says the Qubole roles at its customers had been dormant since the spring, and that it had already flagged one as unused for months yet able to reach sensitive data.

The calls came from the OVH server and Mullvad exits, not from AWS addresses. On October 18 one Mullvad address assumed roles across multiple victims in quick succession, and Sentra puts the time to assume each role at about a second.

AWS told The Register and BleepingComputer that its services "were not compromised by this issue and operated as designed." That is AWS's statement, not Wiz's.

== What the External ID is for: the confused deputy problem
layout: background
source: AWS IAM User Guide, the confused deputy problem | https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/confused-deputy.html
credit: Also from AWS, access to AWS accounts owned by third parties
A vendor with many customers could be tricked by one into using its access to another's. A unique External ID per customer prevents that. AWS does not treat it as a secret. How the actor held valid ones is undisclosed.
notes:
Background, from AWS's IAM User Guide, in general terms.

AWS describes the confused deputy problem as an entity without permission to perform an action coercing a more privileged entity to perform it. In its example, a vendor monitors many customers' accounts. Another customer, who has learned or guessed your role's ARN, which is not a secret, gives that ARN to the vendor as if it were its own. The vendor, acting as a deputy, then uses its access on your account.

The External ID is AWS's answer. It is an optional identifier in the role's trust policy, and its primary function is to address and prevent the confused deputy problem. The vendor generates a unique value for each customer and always includes that customer's value when it assumes the customer's role. The other customer cannot control which value the vendor sends, so the call into your account fails.

AWS says it does not treat the external ID as a secret: anyone with permission to view the role can see it.

This slide explains the design only. The page says how the actor held a valid External ID for each role has not been disclosed.

Also from AWS, access to AWS accounts owned by third parties: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/id_roles_common-scenarios_third-party.html

== Mapping each account with the role's own permissions
layout: stage
stage: 04
phase: Discovery
technique: T1580 | Cloud Infrastructure Discovery
technique: T1619 | Cloud Storage Object Discovery
fact: Storage and data | buckets, objects, databases, tables and clusters
fact: Compute, databases | instances, images, networks, database instances and snapshots
fact: Identity and keys | roles, KMS keys, aliases and key policies
fact: Detect | List and Describe bursts from a vendor role that had been idle
What came back depended on how much each customer had granted Qubole.
notes:
Wiz says the actor enumerated broadly across services once inside. For storage and data it called ListBuckets, ListObjects, GetDatabases, ListTables and DescribeClusters. For compute it called DescribeInstances, DescribeImages, DescribeSecurityGroups and DescribeVpcs, and for databases DescribeDBInstances and DescribeDBSnapshots. For identity and keys it called ListRoles, ListKeys, ListAliases, DescribeKey and GetKeyPolicy.

Every call ran with the integration role's own permissions, so what came back depended on how much each customer had granted Qubole.

ATT&CK spreads this across several techniques, and the page maps all four. Cloud Infrastructure Discovery, T1580, covers the instances, images, networks and snapshots. Cloud Storage Object Discovery, T1619, covers the bucket and object listings. Cloud Service Discovery, T1526, covers the sweep across services. Account Discovery: Cloud Account, T1087.004, covers the role listing. The slide shows the first two.

The page's detection for this stage is a burst of List and Describe calls from a vendor role session in an account where the role had been idle.

== Secrets from Lambda, Secrets Manager and Parameter Store
layout: stage
stage: 05
phase: Credential Access
technique: T1555.006 | Cloud Secrets Management Stores
technique: T1552 | Unsecured Credentials
fact: Lambda | environment variables, via GetFunctionConfiguration
fact: Secrets Manager | ListSecrets, then GetSecretValue
fact: SSM Parameter Store | DescribeParameters and GetParameter
fact: Why it worked | the role could read secrets the integration had no need for
Wiz says integration roles are often granted this access for convenience.
notes:
Wiz describes three routes. In Lambda the actor listed functions and called GetFunctionConfiguration on each, which returns the function's environment variables. In Secrets Manager it called ListSecrets, then GetSecretValue. In SSM Parameter Store it called DescribeParameters and GetParameter.

Wiz says integration roles are often granted this kind of access, along with broad S3 read, for convenience. The Hacker News reports that Qubole's setup guide includes a sample S3 policy that lets the role read every bucket in the account, and Qubole's own documentation for creating the cross-account role shows it: the sample grants s3:GetObject and s3:ListBucket on every resource.

The page maps Secrets Manager and Parameter Store to T1555.006, Credentials from Password Stores: Cloud Secrets Management Stores. Lambda environment variables, and the EC2 user data Sentra reports, fit T1552, Unsecured Credentials, with no sub-technique that matches exactly.

Sentra reports more about this stage than Wiz does. The next slide keeps those details labelled as Sentra's.

== What Sentra adds about the secrets
layout: compare
col: What Sentra saw | EC2 user data scraped | A second route to Lambda tried when the first was blocked | About 100 passwords and keys read in 7 minutes, from three stores | Thousands of credential records inside downloaded files
col: What leaked, per Sentra | Source-control, identity-provider, CI and database credentials | Monitoring and scanner agent keys | The vendor's own tokens and ExternalIds
These details are Sentra's alone. The ExternalIds were read after the actor was inside, so they do not explain its first entry.
notes:
Everything on this slide is Sentra's, and Wiz does not report it. Say so when you present it.

Sentra saw the actor scrape EC2 user data, the startup scripts where tokens and passwords are often left. It saw the actor try a second route to Lambda when the first was blocked, without naming that route. It counts about 100 passwords and keys read in seven minutes from three different stores, plus thousands of credential records inside files the actor downloaded.

By Sentra's account, those included source-control, identity-provider, CI and database credentials, monitoring and scanner agent keys, and the vendor's own tokens and ExternalIds. CI means continuous integration, the pipelines that build and test code.

One caution the page adds. Sentra says the actor read the vendor's own tokens and ExternalIds out of victims' Secrets Manager. That happened after the actor was inside, so it does not explain how the actor held a valid External ID for each role in the first place.

In Sentra's words, "every secret that leaked came from permissions Qubole never needed."

== TruffleHog tested the victims' credentials against AWS, Entra ID and Okta
layout: stage
stage: 06
phase: Initial Access
technique: T1078.004 | Valid Accounts: Cloud Accounts
technique: T1588.002 | Obtain Capabilities: Tool
fact: AWS | GetCallerIdentity with discovered IAM user keys
fact: Entra ID | service principal sign-ins with discovered client secrets
fact: Okta | OAuth token requests with discovered client credentials
fact: Outcome | not reported by any source
The credentials tested here belonged to the victims, not the vendor.
notes:
The credentials tested in this step belonged to the victims, not the vendor. Wiz says TruffleHog called AWS GetCallerIdentity with discovered IAM user keys, checking keys from several AWS accounts in bursts lasting seconds. TruffleHog also signed in as Entra ID service principals using discovered client secrets, and requested OAuth tokens using discovered Okta client credentials.

No source says whether any of these checks succeeded, which Entra ID or Okta endpoints were called, or what was done with a credential that worked. Keep that gap when you tell it.

Wiz's indicator table lists the TruffleHog user agent against AWS GetCallerIdentity and Entra ID sign-ins only, while its text adds Okta, so the page says to check Okta's logs as well. Wiz saw the TruffleHog user agent from October 16 to October 19. Its detection pairs the user agent with one of the actor's addresses, and it notes that legitimate security teams run TruffleHog too.

The page's detection for this stage: the TruffleHog user agent from an indicator address, in CloudTrail and in identity provider sign-in logs.

== Whole buckets, then database backups
layout: stage
stage: 07
phase: Collection
technique: T1530 | Data from Cloud Storage
fact: Sequence | ListBuckets; ListObjects and HeadObject; then GetObject (Wiz)
fact: Scale, per Wiz | whole buckets in some sessions
fact: Taken, per Sentra | customer-profile files, then production database backups
fact: Logging gap | no S3 data events or access logs in several environments (Wiz)
Sentra counts millions of files taken at one company. The data went straight from S3 to the actor's own hosts.
notes:
Wiz saw a consistent sequence: ListBuckets, then ListObjects and HeadObject, then GetObject. Wiz says the actor appears to have been opportunistic, sometimes taking a whole bucket in one session. The page maps this to T1530, Data from Cloud Storage.

Sentra describes an initial theft of thousands of customer-profile files, bulk downloads that ran in the background over the following days, and a final pass from a different VPN address that pulled production database backups. Across one company's accounts, Sentra says, the downloads came to millions of files. It saw no write actions at all. Keep each of those details with Sentra's name.

Wiz points out that in several affected environments, missing logs meant responders could not see which objects the actor read. The next slide sets out what each record shows.

The data went straight from S3 to the actor's own hosts, so the page maps no separate exfiltration technique.

== Which objects left? What each record shows
layout: table
row: Record | What it shows, per the page
row: CloudTrail management events | The role assumption and bucket listing, not object reads (Wiz)
row: S3 data events or access logs | Object reads; missing in several affected environments (Wiz)
row: KMS decrypt records | Downloads of objects encrypted with KMS keys (Sentra)
row: Default S3 encryption | No decrypt record, so the real number is likely higher (Sentra)
row: An S3 Bucket Key | KMS events log the bucket's ARN, not each object (AWS)
A missing decrypt record does not show that an object stayed put.
notes:
Seeing which objects left took more than CloudTrail's defaults.

Wiz points out that CloudTrail management events record the role assumption and the bucket listing but not the object reads. In several affected environments, missing logs meant responders could not see which objects the actor read.

Sentra rebuilt the picture from S3 access logs and, for objects encrypted with KMS keys, matched the downloads against KMS decrypt records. KMS is AWS Key Management Service.

Objects under default S3 encryption leave no such record, Sentra notes, so the real number taken is likely higher, and a missing decrypt record does not show an object stayed put.

On a bucket that uses an S3 Bucket Key, AWS's documentation adds, KMS events in CloudTrail log the bucket's ARN rather than each object's. So even KMS-encrypted objects may not show up one by one.

The lesson for a responder: an empty KMS search does not clear an object. For S3 object reads, the page says, you need data events or server access logs.

== From a vendor's account to customers' data, as the page describes it
layout: layers
layer: Vendor account | 805246085872 | Two IAM users
layer: Customer role | Trusts it with an External ID; no network condition
layer: What it read | Lambda, Secrets Manager, SSM | S3 objects
layer: Where it went | Secrets tested with TruffleHog; S3 data to the actor's hosts
What came back depended on how much each customer had granted Qubole.
notes:
This slide draws the path the page describes, from one vendor's account to its customers' data. Each tier is a step the page states.

At the top is the AWS account Wiz describes as associated with Qubole, with the two IAM users the actor held. One of them also carries legitimate Qubole platform traffic.

Below it is the customer's integration role. Its trust policy let the vendor account assume it when the caller presented an External ID, and it had no condition on where the caller could be. Most of these roles were dormant, and the actor called from an OVH server and VPN exits, not from AWS addresses.

Next is what the role could read. Wiz says integration roles are often granted Lambda configuration, Secrets Manager, SSM parameters and broad S3 read for convenience, and what came back depended on how much each customer had granted Qubole.

Last is where it went: harvested secrets tested with TruffleHog against AWS, Entra ID and Okta, and S3 data taken straight to the actor's own hosts.

AWS says its services were not compromised by this issue and operated as designed. The defenses that follow work on each tier.

== Respond: four defenses against seven stages
layout: matrix
columns: 01 | 02 | 03 | 04 | 05 | 06 | 07
row: Hunt CloudTrail from October 1 with Wiz's indicators | 01 | 02 | 03 | 06
row: Follow every session to the end | 05 | 06 | 07
row: Take the vendor out of the trust policy the same day | 01 | 03 | 05
row: Rotate what the role could read, and revoke its sessions | 03 | 05 | 06
A cell is marked where the defense's own words name something from that stage.
notes:
The page gives nine defenses. This matrix sets the first four, which respond to an intrusion, against the seven stages. The next slide covers the five that harden an estate.

The hunt names stage 01's addresses, stage 02's two IAM users, stage 03's AssumeRole events and quiam- session names, and the TruffleHog user agent that the page ties to stage 06.

Following every session to the end names Secrets Manager, Parameter Store and Lambda from stage 05, identity provider sign-in logs and the TruffleHog user agent from stage 06, and S3 object reads from stage 07. Following a session would pass through stage 04's enumeration on the way, but the defense names the stores and S3, not the List and Describe calls, so column 04 stays empty.

Taking the vendor out of the trust policy names the trust from stage 03, rotating the secrets that were read from stage 05, and blocking the attacker's IP addresses from stage 01.

Rotating and revoking names the role's sessions from stage 03, the secrets, parameters and Lambda variables from stage 05, and the Entra ID and Okta client credentials that stage 06 tested.

== Harden: five defenses against seven stages
layout: matrix
columns: 01 | 02 | 03 | 04 | 05 | 06 | 07
row: Delete unused roles; inventory external trust | 03
row: Limit who can assume a role, and from where | 03
row: Grant only what the integration needs | 05 | 07
row: Log object reads; alert on new networks | 03 | 07
row: For vendors: short-lived credentials | 02 | 03
A cell is marked where the page ties the defense to that stage. None names stage 04, and Wiz does not say IAM user keys were the cause.
notes:
The last five defenses harden an estate before anything happens.

Deleting unused roles answers stage 03, where most of the roles assumed were dormant. Limiting who can assume a role, and from where, answers stage 03 too: Wiz says a source IP condition alone would have blocked every role assumption it observed. Its column 02 stays empty because the page's reading is that a narrowed principal would likely not have stopped an actor using the vendor's own IAM users.

Giving vendor roles only what they need names the secrets stores from stage 05 and the S3 read used at stage 07. Logging object reads names stage 07, and alerting on a vendor assuming a role from a new network names stage 03.

The last row is Wiz's advice to SaaS providers. It names long-lived IAM user keys, the kind of identity stage 02 used, and source IP restrictions and AssumeRole monitoring, which bear on stage 03. Wiz does not say long-lived keys are how the actor got in, so read the mark at 02 as the identity it names, not as a cause.

Column 04 is empty in both matrices. No defense on the page names the enumeration. That gap is a discussion question at the end.

== Hunt every account with a Qubole role from October 1, 2026
layout: table
row: Indicator | What to look for
row: 805246085872 (Wiz) | AssumeRole where sourceIPAddress is not an AWS address
row: IAM users (Wiz) | AIDA3W7DIFLYOLTK63SFS: any activity. AIDAJYRRH5Y3A7AAJM2HW: only from outside AWS, as Qubole uses it too
row: Session names (Wiz) | Beginning quiam-
row: OVH addresses (Wiz) | 57.129.54[.]120; 2001:41d0:70d[:]7800::
row: Mullvad exits (Wiz) | 45.83.220[.]211, 185.209.199[.]73, 89.37.63[.]175; shared, so one alone is not conclusive
row: User agents (Wiz) | Boto3, aws-cli, TruffleHog; use only beside another indicator
notes:
Hunt CloudTrail from October 1, 2026, in every account that holds a Qubole integration role. That is Wiz's window. Wiz gives no reason for the start date, which comes before both clusters of activity.

Wiz's indicators: AssumeRole events where userIdentity.accountId is 805246085872 and sourceIPAddress is not an AWS address; any activity by AIDA3W7DIFLYOLTK63SFS; AIDAJYRRH5Y3A7AAJM2HW from addresses outside AWS, since it also carries legitimate Qubole traffic; and role session names beginning quiam-.

The five addresses are 57.129.54[.]120, 2001:41d0:70d[:]7800::, 45.83.220[.]211, 185.209.199[.]73 and 89.37.63[.]175. Wiz notes that the three Mullvad addresses are shared VPN exits, so activity from one of them alone is not conclusive.

Wiz also lists user agents beginning Boto3/1.37.9, the most common, Boto3/1.43.113, Boto3/1.43.108 and aws-cli/2.37.12, each given in full in its table, and TruffleHog. Ordinary tooling sends the same strings, so use them only alongside the addresses, principal IDs or another of Wiz's indicators.

Sentra's advice is to preserve the logs before they expire.

== Follow every session to the end; cut the trust the same day
layout: compare
col: Follow every session to the end | Into Secrets Manager, Parameter Store, Lambda and S3 | For S3 object reads: data events or server access logs | Check IdP sign-in logs, Entra ID and Okta, for TruffleHog
col: Sentra's same-day actions | Remove Qubole from every role that trusts it | Rotate every secret that was read | Block the attacker's addresses; preserve logs; tell Qubole
An empty KMS search does not clear objects under default S3 encryption, or in a bucket with an S3 Bucket Key.
notes:
Two response defenses, in the order the page gives them.

Follow every session you find to the end. Wiz's second step is to review all activity from each role session the actor opened, especially into Secrets Manager, Parameter Store, Lambda and S3. For S3 that takes data events or server access logs, because management events show the listing but not the object reads. Sentra used S3 access logs, and KMS decrypt records for objects encrypted with KMS keys, to establish which objects left. Objects under default S3 encryption leave no decrypt record, and a bucket with an S3 Bucket Key logs the bucket rather than each object, so an empty KMS search does not clear them. Then, as Wiz advises, check your identity provider's sign-in logs, Entra ID and Okta included, for the TruffleHog user agent, because the credentials tested there came out of your own secrets.

Take the vendor out of the trust policy the same day. The right column is Sentra's five same-day actions. Wiz says to remove the trust for integrations you no longer use, and both say to rotate what was read. An AWS spokesperson gave similar advice: review cross-account IAM roles, remove unused integrations, grant least privilege, add source IP conditions, and follow AWS's guidance for potentially compromised credentials.

== Rotate and revoke, then delete the roles you no longer use
layout: compare
col: Rotate, and revoke as a separate step | Every secret, parameter and Lambda variable the role could read | Revoke the role's sessions: rotation does not end them | Remove the trust before revoking, or in the same change | Long-lived keys given to Qubole: deactivate, review, then delete
col: Delete roles you no longer use | Wiz: inventory external trust; remove unused integration roles | This page's suggestion: IAM Access Analyzer's external access findings | This page's reading: alert on any assumption of an idle role
notes:
Rotate everything the role could read, and revoke its sessions separately. Wiz's instruction is that if the actor assumed an integration role, you rotate every secret, parameter and Lambda environment variable the role could read and revoke its active sessions, because rotating a credential does not end temporary sessions already issued. In IAM, revoking attaches a policy that denies credentials issued before that moment. It reaches only those earlier sessions: while the role still trusts the vendor account, the actor can assume it again, so remove the trust first or in the same change. Rotate any Entra ID or Okta client credential in those stores too. If you ever gave Qubole long-lived access keys, the kind the separate October 8 to 11 activity used, deactivate them first, review their last-used data and CloudTrail history, and only then delete them.

Wiz's hardening steps start with an inventory of external trust and the removal of unused integration roles. IAM Access Analyzer's external access findings, the page's own suggestion rather than the researchers', flag roles whose trust policies allow principals outside your account or organization. Because the actor's requests were the only vendor role assumptions that month in a majority of the environments Wiz saw, the page says an idle role's assumption deserves an alert on its own.

== Limit who can assume a vendor role, from where, and to what
layout: compare
col: Limit who, and from where | Wiz, where supported: narrow the principal to the identity that needs it | Wiz: aws:SourceIp or aws:SourceVpce, limited to the vendor's ranges | Treat the External ID as an identifier; rotate it with the vendor
col: Only what the integration needs | Wiz calls for least privilege | This page's reading: no secrets stores or Lambda configuration; S3 read scoped | Check the sample S3 policy you copied at setup
This page's reading: a narrowed principal would likely not have stopped this actor, which used the vendor's own IAM users.
notes:
Limit who can assume a vendor role, and from where. Where the vendor supports it, Wiz recommends narrowing the trust policy's principal from the vendor's whole account to the identity that needs it, and an aws:SourceIp or aws:SourceVpce condition limited to the vendor's documented network ranges. Wiz says a source IP condition alone would have blocked every role assumption it observed; Sentra says, separately, that a network restriction alone would have blocked every login it saw. The page's own reading is that a narrowed principal would likely not have stopped this actor, which used the vendor's own IAM users. Wiz also advises rotating the External ID with the vendor, with a distinct value per role where supported.

Give vendor roles only what the integration needs. Wiz notes that Lambda configuration, Secrets Manager, SSM parameters and broad S3 read are often granted to integration roles for convenience, and calls for least privilege. The page's reading, not Wiz's wording, is to keep Secrets Manager, Parameter Store and Lambda configuration access off integration roles, and scope S3 read to the buckets the service uses. By Sentra's account, none of the leaked secrets needed to be readable by the integration. The Hacker News reports, and Qubole's documentation shows, a sample S3 policy that lets the role read every bucket.

== Log object reads, and what to do if you are the vendor
layout: compare
col: Log reads; alert on new networks | An organization trail | S3 data events or server access logs on buckets that matter | Alert when a vendor assumes a role from an unusual network
col: If you are the vendor, per Wiz | Assume customer roles from workload roles with short-lived credentials | Restrict principals by source IP or VPC | Publish egress ranges; watch AssumeRole from unexpected networks
Here the actor called from an OVH server and VPN exits, while the vendor's own traffic came from AWS addresses.
notes:
Log object reads, and alert on vendor role assumptions from new networks. Wiz recommends an organization trail plus S3 data events or server access logs on buckets that matter, and an alert when a vendor's account assumes a role from a network it does not normally use. Here the actor called from an OVH server and VPN exits, while the vendor's own traffic came from AWS addresses.

If your platform assumes customer roles, do it with short-lived credentials. Wiz's advice to SaaS providers is to assume customer roles from workload roles with short-lived credentials rather than long-lived IAM user keys, restrict principals by source IP or VPC, publish egress ranges so customers can pin them, and watch your own CloudTrail for AssumeRole calls into customer accounts from unexpected networks.

Wiz does not say that long-lived keys are how the actor got the vendor's credentials. Present this as the safer design, not as the cause.

== Check your own environment
layout: checklist
check: Can you list every role that trusts an account outside your organization?
check: Which vendor roles have sat idle for months, and would an assumption alert anyone?
check: Do your vendor trust policies limit the network a caller can come from?
check: Can a vendor role read Secrets Manager, Parameter Store, Lambda configuration or every bucket?
check: Would your logs show which S3 objects a role read last month?
check: Could you rotate everything a vendor role could read, and revoke its sessions, today?
check: Are long-lived access keys you once gave a vendor still active?
notes:
Turn the page's defenses into questions about your own estate. Each question comes from one of them.

Ask whether you could list every role that trusts an account outside your account or organization, and who still uses each. Ask which vendor roles have sat idle for months, and whether an assumption of one would alert anyone.

Ask whether your vendor trust policies carry a network condition, such as aws:SourceIp or aws:SourceVpce, limited to the vendor's documented ranges.

Ask whether any vendor role can read Secrets Manager, Parameter Store, Lambda configuration, or every bucket in the account, and whether the integration needs it.

Ask whether you log S3 data events or server access logs on the buckets that matter, so you could say which objects a role read.

Ask whether you could rotate every secret, parameter and Lambda variable a vendor role could read, and revoke its sessions, with the trust removed in the same change.

Finally, ask whether long-lived access keys you once gave a vendor are still active, the kind the separate October 8 to 11 activity used.

== Every request carried a valid External ID. What would have stopped it?
layout: question
Every AssumeRole request Wiz attributed to the actor carried an External ID and succeeded, using the vendor's own IAM users. What condition in your vendor trust policies would refuse a caller like that?
notes:
Give the room a minute with this one.

Remind the group of the facts. Every AssumeRole request Wiz attributed to the actor carried an External ID, and every one succeeded. Sentra says the attacker presented the right one every time, and how the actor held a valid External ID for each role has not been disclosed. From October 18 the actor was using an IAM user that Qubole's own platform also uses. The calls came from an OVH server and Mullvad VPN exits, not from AWS addresses.

Ask each person to look at one vendor trust policy in their own estate, or one they remember, and say which of its conditions would have refused this caller. Then show the answer slide.

== The control Wiz and Sentra say would have blocked all they saw
layout: answer
The network condition is the only control Wiz and Sentra say would have blocked everything they saw.
- Wiz: aws:SourceIp or aws:SourceVpce, limited to the vendor's documented ranges.
- The page's reading: a narrowed principal likely would not have stopped it.
notes:
The page's answer is the network.

Wiz says a source IP condition alone would have blocked every role assumption it observed, and Sentra says, separately, that a network restriction alone would have blocked every login it saw. Wiz recommends an aws:SourceIp or aws:SourceVpce condition limited to the vendor's documented network ranges. That needs the vendor to publish the addresses it calls from, which Wiz asks SaaS providers to do.

The External ID was not designed to stop this. AWS's IAM User Guide says AWS does not treat the external ID as a secret; its purpose is the confused deputy problem.

Narrowing the principal from the vendor's whole account to one identity is also Wiz's advice. The page's own reading, not the researchers', is that it would likely not have stopped this actor, which used the vendor's own IAM users, one of them also used by Qubole's platform.

Bring the group's answers back to this. The conditions in place checked who was calling, and the actor satisfied them. By both researchers' accounts, a check on where the call came from would have blocked what they saw.

== What would you add for stage 04?
layout: question
No defense on the page names stage 04, where the actor enumerated storage, compute, databases, IAM roles and KMS keys with the role's own permissions. What would you add, and which reported detail would it catch?
notes:
This question comes from the empty column in both defense matrices.

At stage 04 the actor listed buckets, objects, databases and tables, described clusters, instances, images, security groups, VPCs, database instances and snapshots, and listed roles, keys and aliases. Every call ran with the integration role's own permissions, so what came back depended on how much each customer had granted Qubole.

Let the group propose ideas before you show the answer slide. Ask each proposal to name the detail from the page that it would have caught, and whether it would have fired before the secrets were read at stage 05.

== The page ties no defense to stage 04
layout: answer
Any proposal is your own. Stage 04's facts box on the page gives one detection:
- A burst of List and Describe calls from a vendor role session in an account where the role had been idle.
notes:
Be clear that the page stops here. No defense names stage 04, and this deck adds none of its own.

The page's facts box for the stage does give a detection: a burst of List and Describe calls from a vendor role session in an account where the role had been idle. Test each proposal against it, and against the reported calls: bucket and object listings, database and table listings, cluster, instance, image, security group, VPC and snapshot descriptions, and role, key and alias listings.

The group may argue that a page defense tied to stage 03 reaches this stage first: an assumption of a role that has been idle deserves an alert on its own. Or that giving vendor roles only what the integration needs shrinks what enumeration returns. Those arguments are the group's own. No defense on the page names stage 04, though the stage itself notes that what came back depended on what each customer had granted.

== Primary sources
layout: links
link: Wiz, Trusted by Default: Qubole cross-account access abused in the wild | https://www.wiz.io/blog/qubole-cross-account-access-abused-in-the-wild
link: Sentra, Inside the Qubole supply chain attack | https://sentra.io/blog/breaches/inside-the-qubole-supply-chain-attack
link: The Register, with AWS's statement | https://www.theregister.com/security/2026/11/09/attackers-backdoor-trusted-aws-integrations-to-steal-secrets/5308553
link: BleepingComputer, with AWS's statement | https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/hackers-exploit-trusted-aws-access-to-breach-multiple-cloud-environments/
link: The Hacker News, access-key onboarding and the sample S3 policy | https://thehackernews.com/2026/11/hackers-exploit-quboles-trusted-aws.html
link: SecurityWeek, reporting | https://www.securityweek.com/over-a-dozen-organizations-hit-in-qubole-supply-chain-attack/
link: Qubole, creating a cross-account IAM role (sample S3 policy) | https://docs.qubole.com/en/latest/user-guide/managing-accounts/aws/iam-roles/create-iam-roles.html
link: Idera, Idera divests Qubole (2025) | https://www.ideracorp.com/pressreleases/divests-qubole
notes:
These are the primary sources the csoh.org kill chain cites, over two slides. Every fact about the incident in this deck comes from that page, which draws on these.

Wiz's post is the main account: the role assumptions, the indicators and the defenses. Sentra detected the activity, and its post carries the details labelled as Sentra's in this deck. The Register and BleepingComputer carry AWS's statement. The Hacker News notes that how the actor got the credentials is undisclosed, that Qubole also supports onboarding with access keys, and the sample S3 policy. SecurityWeek reported the attack. Qubole's own documentation shows the sample S3 policy. Idera's release covers its sale of Qubole.

Disclosure, once more: Google acquired Wiz in March 2026, and the site's author works at Wiz. Mandiant, which investigated with Wiz and Sentra, is part of Google.

== Primary sources, continued
layout: links
link: Catalin Cimpanu, n0n's claim | https://bsky.app/profile/campuscodi.risky.biz/post/3n22h4dxfck2x
link: Check Point Cyberint, threat actor investigation: n0n | https://cyberint.com/blog/threat-intelligence/threat-actor-investigation-n0n/
link: AWS, access to AWS accounts owned by third parties (External ID) | https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/id_roles_common-scenarios_third-party.html
link: AWS, revoke IAM role temporary security credentials | https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/id_roles_use_revoke-sessions.html
link: AWS, IAM Access Analyzer | https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/what-is-access-analyzer.html
link: AWS, S3 Bucket Keys (CloudTrail logs the bucket ARN) | https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/bucket-key.html
link: TechTarget, Idera's Qubole acquisition (2020) | https://www.techtarget.com/searchdatamanagement/news/252490555/Idera-targets-cloud-data-lake-market-with-Qubole-acquisition
link: MITRE ATT&CK T1199 | https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1199/
notes:
Catalin Cimpanu reported n0n's claim, and Check Point's Cyberint team investigated n0n and found no Qubole-specific markers in the files released.

The four AWS pages cover the External ID, revoking a role's temporary credentials, IAM Access Analyzer, and S3 Bucket Keys, under which CloudTrail logs the bucket ARN rather than each object. TechTarget reported Idera's acquisition of Qubole in 2020. The MITRE page defines T1199, Trusted Relationship, the technique behind stage 03.

The page also cites a later BleepingComputer report on n0n's data claims and the suspected Qubole link. It is left off these slides because its address names companies whose link to this intrusion nobody has confirmed.

The background slides name their own sources, all from AWS's documentation.

